uity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317401 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317401 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317401 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317401 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317401 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317401 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317401 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ 2, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317401 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d801752-c780-5add-95cf-b3a0d0a59bd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317401 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot 1}{2}) \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375031 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375031 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375031 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375031 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-26, & x\\in [-5,0) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=5.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375031 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae903844-3154-5fe9-a420-6a9e8272ce91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375031 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=5.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(5) & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)=-3(5)=-15 \\\\ {f}^{+}(5) & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={5}^{2}-26=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(5)+{f}^{+}(5)}{2}=\\frac{-15+(-1)}{2}=-8.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375032 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375032 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375032 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375032 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375032 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "948a4c6b-6866-5b53-840b-620e511c4ece" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375033 #edb/ref 1080 "-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375033 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375033 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4dcce98c-8760-56f2-b17b-5fd945b150e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf7482ba-c42e-5af4-af91-a8b902345ce3"